'''''Chamaecyparis''''', common name '''false-cypress''' (to distinguish it from related true [[cypress]]es), also simply called '''cypress''', is a [[genus]] of [[Pinophyta|conifers]] in the cypress [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] [[Cupressaceae]], [[native plant|native]] to eastern Asia ([[Japan]] and [[Taiwan]]) and to the western and eastern margins of the United States.<ref name=m>{{cite web |url=http://wcsp.science.kew.org/namedetail.do?name_id=382810 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191018125850/https://wcsp.science.kew.org/namedetail.do?name_id=382810 |url-status=dead |archive-date=October 18, 2019 |title=''Chamaecyparis'' |work=[[World Checklist of Selected Plant Families]] (WCSP) |publisher=[[Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew]] }}</ref> The name is derived from the [[Greek language|Greek]] ''khamai'' (χαμαί), meaning "on the earth", and ''kuparissos'' (κυπάρισσος) for "cypress".<ref>{{Cite web|title=Chamaecyparis obtusa - Plant Finder|url=http://www.missouribotanicalgarden.org/PlantFinder/PlantFinderDetails.aspx?kempercode=c204|access-date=2021-02-18|website=www.missouribotanicalgarden.org}}</ref>

They are medium-sized to large [[tree]]s growing from {{convert|20|-|70|m|0|abbr=on}} tall, with foliage in flat sprays. The [[leaf|leaves]] are of two types, needle-like juvenile leaves on young seedlings up to a year old, and scale-like adult leaves. The [[conifer cone|cones]] are globose to oval, with 8-14 scales arranged in opposite [[decussation|decussate]] pairs; each scale bears 2-4 small [[seed]]s.

